## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform design, policy-as-code, drift handling, and test automation at scale—core for Oracle, Tesla, and LinkedIn-like environments.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliasing for multi-region/multi-cloud setups
- Wrapper modules to standardize tagging and policy constraints
- Terraform Cloud/Sentinel or OPA for governance
- Drift detection workflows and isolated drift environments
- End-to-end testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example

```hcl
# Wrapper module injecting standard tags
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
variable "tags" { type = map(string) }

module "service" {
  source = "../aws-azure-base"
  providers = {
    aws   = aws.us_east_1
    aws_eu = aws.eu_west_1
    azurerm = azurerm.westus2
  }
  tags = merge(var.tags, {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you structure a Sentinel policy to enforce tag requirements and deny secrets in state?
- How would you simulate drift in tests and verify policy compliance across clouds?

## Why This Is Asked
- Tests for for_each on maps, dynamic blocks, and policy handling with Terraform. 
- Checks ability to compose minimal wrapper modules that feed into a shared VPC module without duplicating resources.
- Validates exporting a clean map of per-env ARNs for use by the VPC module.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform maps and for_each
- aws_iam_role and aws_iam_role_policy
- jsonencode for inline policies
- Module outputs and per-env composition

## Code Example
```hcl
variable "env_roles" {
  type = map(object({ enabled=bool, role_name=string, policy_json=string }))
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "env" {
  for_each = { for k,v in var.env_roles : k => v if v.enabled }
  name = each.value.role_name
  assume_role_policy = <<POLICY
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{"Effect": "Allow", "Principal": {"Service": "ec2.amazonaws.com"}, "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"}]
}
POLICY
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "env_inline" {
  for_each = aws_iam_role.env
  role     = each.key
  policy   = each.value.policy_json
}

output "role_arns" {
  value = { for k,v in aws_iam_role.env: k => v.arn }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add basic unit tests for this wrapper using Terraform's validation patterns?
- What are the trade-offs of inline policies vs managed policies in this setup?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-repo data sharing and multi-region provider patterns, plus a practical gating approach to prevent drift.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliasing across regions
- data.terraform_remote_state for read-only cross-repo data
- Per-region backends and workflows
- CI policy gates to enforce drift checks and approvals
- Cross-region resource referencing of VPC/subnet IDs

## Code Example
```javascript
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.3"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "east"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "west"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
data "terraform_remote_state" "net" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "infra-network-bucket"
    key    = "network/us-east-1/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
# usage
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  provider    = aws.west
  ami         = "ami-0abcdef1234567890"
  subnet_id   = data.terraform_remote_state.net.outputs.public_subnet_id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection across repos? 
- How would you handle regional failover and state restoration?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests cross-cloud module design, provider aliasing, and policy-driven governance at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform multi-provider modules
- Provider aliasing and per-tenant scoping
- Open Policy Agent integration with terraform show -json plan
- CI gates and fail-fast

## Code Example
```javascript
// Module interface skeleton (Terraform-like)
module data_lake {
  source = ./modules/data_lake
  tenants = {
    t1 = { cloud = aws, region = us-east-1 }
    t2 = { cloud = gcp, region = us-central1 }
  }
  quotas = {
    t1 = { data_lake_size_gb = 100 }
    t2 = { data_lake_size_gb = 200 }
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Sample OPA policy skeleton
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.resource_changes[_].change.actions[_] == create
}
```

